# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# R-20 — one line ending, everywhere.
#
# Git ALREADY stores every tracked text file with LF: this file changes nothing
# that is committed and rewrites no history. What it adds is the CHECKOUT rule.
# Git for Windows installs with `core.autocrlf=true`, so a clone on Windows
# writes CRLF to disk, and two things quietly depended on that:
#
#   `apps/api/test/google-write-reachability.test.ts` asserts a source snippet
#   that spans a line break, so it fails on a CRLF checkout and passes on LF —
#   a false failure that says nothing about the code under test.
#
#   Prisma stores a checksum of each `migration.sql` in `_prisma_migrations`.
#   A CRLF copy hashes differently from the LF copy a database recorded, so
#   `prisma migrate` reports a mismatch that is only line endings. Three
#   migrations are CRLF in a Windows checkout today.
#
# With `eol=lf` every platform checks the same bytes out, so both stop being
# platform-specific.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Everything Git detects as text: normalised to LF in the repository, and
# written out as LF on every platform, whatever `core.autocrlf` says locally.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Stated explicitly because a checksum is computed over exactly these bytes.
*.sql text eol=lf

# Binary: never inspected for text, never converted.
*.doc binary
*.thmx binary
